    {
      "id": "brienne-duhesme",
      "nationId": "france",
      "name": "Duhesme's Division",
      "formation": "1st Division, II Corps (Victor)",
      "side": "French",
      "summary": "Victor's leading division, which reached the field at half past three and attacked Olsufiev's Russians in the town, fighting them to a standstill for an hour. With no cavalry on its flank it was ridden over by Pahlen's and Vasilchikov's squadrons and lost eight guns; but after dark Huguet-Chateau led four hundred men of the 37th and 56th up an unguarded road into the château, and nearly took Blücher and Gneisenau there.",
      "notes": [
        "Built from A Clash of Eagles' French 1812 list, the nearest to 1814: there is no list for the army Napoleon fought with in France, of the veterans of 1813, conscripts called up that winter and battalions drawn from depots across the Empire.",
        "After Nafziger's return of the army under Napoleon at Châlons on 25 January, four days before the battle.",
        "Each regiment had a single battalion here, of five to nine hundred men; each is shown as its one battalion.",
        "Nafziger gives the division without its brigades. Huguet-Chateau's brigade of the 37th and 56th, which took the château, is from Petre; the 24th Léger and 19th Line are the other, whose commander isn't known.",
        "Nafziger gives the II Corps' batteries together, not by division: four 6-pdr foot batteries, a 12-pdr battery and three horse batteries. One of the 6-pdr foot batteries is with each division here.",
        "Its foot battery, with four battalions where the list wants six for one, shows as a historical departure.",
        "Commanders are at Staff Rating 8, the default for historical forces."
      ],
